          kind: "explain",
          title: "The Monro-Kellie doctrine and compliance",
          body:
            "The adult skull cannot expand. It holds three things: brain tissue, blood, and [[csf|cerebrospinal fluid]]. The [[monro-kellie-doctrine|Monro-Kellie doctrine]] says their total volume is fixed, so if one grows, another must shrink or the pressure rises.\n\nWhen a mass (a tumor, a hematoma, swelling, or blocked CSF in [[hydrocephalus|hydrocephalus]]) starts to grow, the brain compensates. CSF is pushed out of the skull into the spinal sac, and blood is squeezed out of the veins and venous sinuses. During this phase intracranial pressure barely changes.\n\nThat buffer is limited. [[intracranial-compliance|Intracranial compliance]] is how much volume can be added for a given rise in pressure. When the buffer is spent, compliance is low, and each further small addition (a little more bleeding, a rise in carbon dioxide from shallow breathing, a seizure, a fever) raises pressure steeply. This is why patients with [[raised-intracranial-pressure|raised intracranial pressure]] can look stable and then deteriorate within minutes.",
