            mechanism: "The pressure-volume curve is flat at first, then bends sharply upward. On the flat part, CSF displacement into the spinal subarachnoid space and compression of the low-pressure venous system absorb new volume. On the steep part, those reserves are exhausted, so the same small volume produces a much larger pressure rise.",
            compare: "A slowly growing meningioma can reach a large size with few symptoms because there is time to compensate. A smaller acute hematoma can be fatal because it fills the reserve in hours. Speed of growth matters as much as size.",
            deeper: "Carbon dioxide is a powerful dilator of cerebral vessels. Sedation that causes hypoventilation raises arterial carbon dioxide, expands cerebral blood volume, and in a patient on the steep part of the curve can tip pressure up sharply. Hypercapnia and hypoxia are avoided for the same reason.",
