            "Blood flows into the brain because arterial pressure is higher than the pressure inside the skull. [[cerebral-perfusion-pressure|Cerebral perfusion pressure]] (CPP) is the difference:\n\nCPP = mean arterial pressure (MAP) minus intracranial pressure (ICP).\n\nIf ICP rises and MAP does not, CPP falls and the brain becomes ischemic, which causes more swelling and a higher ICP: a vicious circle. Within limits, [[cerebral-autoregulation|cerebral autoregulation]] keeps flow steady when perfusion pressure changes, but injured brain often loses this ability, so flow follows pressure passively. This is also why lowering blood pressure aggressively in a patient with raised ICP can be dangerous: it lowers CPP.\n\nAs ICP approaches arterial pressure and the brainstem is compressed, the body raises blood pressure to keep blood flowing. The [[cushing-response|Cushing response]] is hypertension (often with a widening pulse pressure), bradycardia, and irregular breathing. It is a late sign of brainstem compression and imminent herniation. Do not wait for it: headache, vomiting, [[papilledema|papilledema]], and a falling level of consciousness come earlier.",
