            "Rigid folds of dura divide the skull: the falx cerebri between the hemispheres and the tentorium cerebelli above the cerebellum, with an opening (the tentorial notch) for the midbrain. A mass pushes brain from high pressure toward low, past these edges. This is [[brain-herniation|brain herniation]].\n\n[[uncal-herniation|Uncal herniation]]: a mass in one hemisphere, often temporal, pushes the medial temporal lobe (the uncus) over the tentorial edge. It compresses the [[oculomotor-nerve|third nerve]] on the same side. The pupil fibers run on the outside of the nerve, so an [[ipsilateral|ipsilateral]] dilated, sluggish pupil comes first, then a fixed pupil and a full third nerve palsy (eye down and out, ptosis). Compression of the cerebral peduncle gives [[contralateral|contralateral]] [[hemiparesis|hemiparesis]], and consciousness falls as the midbrain is distorted. Sometimes the midbrain is pushed against the opposite tentorial edge, compressing the other cerebral peduncle and causing hemiparesis on the same side as the mass: the [[kernohan-notch-phenomenon|Kernohan notch phenomenon]], a falsely localizing sign.\n\n[[central-herniation|Central herniation]]: diffuse or midline pressure pushes the diencephalon straight down through the notch. Signs progress from top to bottom: drowsiness, small reactive pupils, and [[cheyne-stokes-respiration|Cheyne-Stokes breathing]] (diencephalon), then mid-position fixed pupils and [[decerebrate-posturing|extensor posturing]] (midbrain and pons), then irregular breathing and loss of remaining reflexes (medulla).\n\n[[subfalcine-herniation|Subfalcine herniation]]: the cingulate gyrus slides under the falx. It is often seen as midline shift on imaging and can compress an anterior cerebral artery, causing leg weakness or infarction.\n\n[[tonsillar-herniation|Tonsillar herniation]]: the cerebellar tonsils are pushed down through the foramen magnum and compress the medulla, causing respiratory arrest and cardiovascular collapse. It is the danger of posterior fossa masses, and the reason lumbar puncture is avoided when a mass with [[mass-effect|mass effect]] is possible.",
