            "[[brain-death|Brain death]], also called death by neurologic criteria, is the permanent loss of function of the whole brain, including the brainstem, in a patient who has coma, has lost brainstem reflexes, and cannot breathe. It is legally and medically death. The 2023 consensus guideline from the American Academy of Neurology, American Academy of Pediatrics, Child Neurology Society, and Society of Critical Care Medicine sets out how it is determined in adults and children.\n\nPrerequisites come first:\n- A known cause that can explain the loss of all brain function (for example a large hemorrhage, severe traumatic injury, or hypoxic-ischemic injury after cardiac arrest), confirmed by history and imaging, and evidence that it is irreversible, including an adequate observation period.\n- Confounders excluded or corrected: sedative and other CNS-depressant drugs (allowing time for clearance), residual neuromuscular blockade, hypothermia, hypotension, and severe metabolic, acid-base, or endocrine disturbances.\n\nThe examination then shows:\n- [[coma|Coma]] with no brain-mediated response to strong stimuli in the face and limbs.\n- Absent brainstem reflexes: pupils fixed to light, no [[oculocephalic-reflex|oculocephalic]] or [[oculovestibular-reflex|oculovestibular]] responses, no [[corneal-reflex|corneal reflex]], no facial movement to noxious stimuli, no [[gag-reflex|gag]] or cough.\n- Apnea: on the [[apnea-test|apnea test]], no breathing effort despite a carbon dioxide rise large enough to drive breathing if the medulla were working.\n\nMovements generated by the spinal cord, such as leg flexion to foot pressure or finger jerks, can persist after brain death and do not exclude it. They need an experienced examiner to tell them apart from brain-mediated responses.",
